course: dictionary
lesson: 4930
title: Imperfect: not perfect, and a past tense
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Imperfect means not perfect: it has faults or mistakes. Everyone and everything can be imperfect, and that is normal.
explain: «Your own imperfect work is worth more than someone else's perfect work with your name on it.» Here imperfect does not mean bad. It means your work has mistakes, but it is honestly yours, and that makes it valuable.
explain: «Broken or imperfect things are not ruined.» Broken is a bigger problem than imperfect, but the sentence says neither one means the thing is finished or useless. The words around a word give you clues, so look at «not ruined» to see the sentence's message.
explain: Imperfect has a second meaning in grammar. The imperfect tense is a past tense for actions that were going on or happened again and again, like «I was reading» in English. Check which meaning fits the sentence before you choose.
fact: Imperfect means having faults or mistakes; not perfect.
fact: «Imperfect» is an adjective, as in «an imperfect world» and «imperfect but useful».
fact: In grammar, «the imperfect» is a noun: the imperfect tense describes past actions that were going on or happened often.
fact: «Perfect» means having no faults, so «imperfect» is its opposite.
fact: The imperfect tense is also used in French and Spanish.
exercise: «Your own imperfect work is worth more than someone else's perfect work with your name on it.» What does imperfect mean here? | Flawless and brilliant | Stolen from someone else | *Having mistakes, but still your own | Finished in a hurry
exercise: «Broken or imperfect things are not ruined.» Which word is the opposite of imperfect? | Useful | Ruined | Broken | *Perfect
exercise: In grammar, what does the imperfect tense describe? | Questions only | Actions that are finished in one moment | *Past actions that were going on or happened often | Actions that will happen in the future
test: What does imperfect mean? | Expensive | *Not perfect, with some faults | Flawless and perfect | Very, very fast
test: Which is an example of the imperfect tense? | He will swim tomorrow | Read the book now | The book will be read | *I was reading when the bell rang
exam: Which sentence uses imperfect to mean having faults? | *Her drawing was imperfect, with a smudge in one corner | The shop was closed on Sunday | The sky was a perfect blue | He will swim tomorrow
